Two separate bugs: df_append = (f'{lookup_id}, {id_number}') builds a single formatted string, it doesn't add a row to anything; and new_df = [] is never appended to in the loop shown. Build a list of row-dicts as you loop, then construct the DataFrame once at the end instead of growing one row-by-row:

rows = []
for item in items:
    id_number = get_score_by_id(df_past, item)
    rows.append({
        "Lookup item": item,
        "Corresponding item": "OK" if id_number is not None else "NAN"
    })
new_df = pd.DataFrame(rows)

(pd.DataFrame.append is removed in modern pandas anyway, so looped appending isn't the right pattern regardless.) Make sure get_score_by_id returns None (not raises or returns empty) when no match exists, since that's what the ternary above depends on to decide "OK" vs "NAN".
